⛔ **Ungraded, unassigned** — `domain:*`, type and priority are triage's to write.

## Finding

`AppPlugin`'s stack-declared security-metadata registrar drops a `permissions` value it cannot read, at both arms, with no log — the SECOND consumer of the two-readings collision #18031 measured, and the one that stays silent after #18031's fix.

`packages/runtime/src/app-plugin.ts`:

```js
const rawSecurityBundle = this.collections.manifest
  ? { ...this.collections.manifest, ...this.collections }
  : this.collections;
// …
for (const [field, type] of SECURITY_FIELDS) {     // ['permissions', 'permission'] among them
  const arr = securityBundle?.[field];
  if (!Array.isArray(arr)) continue;               // arm A-structured → skipped whole, silently
  for (const item of arr) {
    if (!item?.name) continue;                     // arm A-legacy strings → every member dropped, silently
    metadata.registerInMemory(type, item.name, item);
  }
}
```

The flatten is `{ ...manifest, ...collections }`, so the manifest's `permissions` is read **whenever the stack declares no `permissions` collection of its own**. And `ManifestSchema.permissions` is the ADR-0025 §3.2 capability GRANT (`string[]` or `{ services, hooks, network, fs }`), not the ADR-0090 `PermissionSet[]` this loop wants — the same one key / two readings / one slot that #18031 filed:

- structured arm ⇒ an object ⇒ `Array.isArray` false ⇒ `continue`, nothing registered, nothing logged;
- legacy arm ⇒ bare strings ⇒ `!item?.name` ⇒ every member dropped, nothing logged.

## Why it is a finding rather than correct skipping

Skipping is the right OUTCOME; the silence is not. AGENTS.md "Route & surface ownership" §3 — absence must be loud. An author who writes permission sets on the manifest (`permissions: [{ name, isDefault }]`) instead of in `defineStack({ permissions: […] })` gets no set registered, no `sys_audience_binding_suggestion`, and no line anywhere saying why. That is the AI-authoring trap: metadata stored by someone other than its author, silently discarded.

The sibling consumer — `collectDeclaredSuggestions` in `@objectstack/plugin-security` — now names the arm it found, the consequence and the remedy (see the PR for #18031). This site does not, so half the collision is still silent, and this is the half that runs at every boot for every stack.

## Suggested scope (⛔ not a ruling)

Mirror the repaired shape: classify the value, and report once per boot naming which arm was found, what is lost if the author meant permission sets, and that the sets belong in the package's own stack collection. `ctx.logger.warn` is already in hand three lines up. ⛔ Do not make the loop tolerant of either arm — the readings are incompatible and widening the key was rejected by name (#14242 road C, maintainer 2026-09-02).

The same loop's other four fields (`positions`, `capabilities`, `sharingRules`) share the `!item?.name` line; whether they can be reached by a colliding manifest key is unmeasured here and is the first thing to check.

Refs: #18031 · #11333 · ADR-0025 · ADR-0090 · ADR-0130 D4

